<p><em>Airspace • Sectional Charts • NOTAMs • TFRs • Airport Operations • Collision Avoidance</em></p>
<p><strong>2026 EDITION  </strong>Built from the National Airspace System course presentation. Current FAA sources were used to verify airspace terminology, authorization requirements, NOTAM/TFR resources, and chart information.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-use-this-chapter"><strong>How to Use This Chapter</strong></h2>
<p>Airspace questions are among the most visual questions on the FAA Remote Pilot knowledge test. You will often be given a sectional-chart excerpt and asked to determine the airspace, identify an airport or obstruction, interpret a symbol, or decide whether authorization is required. The goal is not to memorize a collection of colored lines. The goal is to look at a location and answer three questions: What airspace am I in? What restrictions or hazards affect it? What must I do before I fly?</p>
<h2 id="learning-objectives"><strong>Learning Objectives</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>Describe the National Airspace System and distinguish controlled from uncontrolled airspace.</li>
<li>Identify Classes B, C, D, E, and G airspace on a sectional chart and determine when Part 107 airspace authorization is required.</li>
<li>Recognize prohibited, restricted, warning, military operations, alert, and other special-use airspace.</li>
<li>Use sectional charts, the Chart Supplement, NOTAMs, and TFR information during preflight planning.</li>
<li>Interpret latitude and longitude, airport symbols, runway information, obstacles, and common sectional-chart markings.</li>
<li>Explain airport traffic patterns, runway numbering, SIDA concepts, and UAS responsibilities near airports.</li>
<li>Apply collision-avoidance principles and recognize common inflight hazards.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="2-1-the-national-airspace-system"><strong>2.1 The National Airspace System</strong></h2>
<p>The National Airspace System (NAS) is the network of airspace, airports, navigation systems, air traffic control facilities, procedures, and regulations that allows aviation to function safely in the United States. For a remote pilot, understanding the NAS means more than knowing whether a drone is below 400 feet. The pilot must understand what airspace exists at the aircraft&#x27;s actual altitude, what nearby crewed aircraft may be doing, and whether temporary restrictions change the normal operating environment.</p>
<p>The course presentation divides airspace into regulatory and non-regulatory categories and also emphasizes the practical distinction between controlled and uncontrolled airspace. Controlled airspace includes Classes A, B, C, D, and E. Class G is uncontrolled. &#x27;Uncontrolled&#x27; does not mean unregulated; Part 107 rules still apply in Class G.</p>
<p><strong>EXAM STRATEGY  </strong>For almost every airspace problem, ask: (1) What class of airspace exists at my planned altitude? (2) Does Part 107 require FAA authorization there? (3) Is there a NOTAM, TFR, special-use area, or other restriction that changes the answer?</p>
<h2 id="2-2-controlled-airspace-and-part-107-authorization"><strong>2.2 Controlled Airspace and Part 107 Authorization</strong></h2>
<p>For Part 107 pilots, the most important authorization rule is straightforward: prior FAA authorization is required before operating in Class B, Class C, Class D, or within the lateral boundaries of the surface area of Class E airspace designated for an airport. Many routine authorizations can be obtained through LAANC. Class E that begins at 700 or 1,200 feet AGL is different: a drone operating below that floor may still be in Class G and therefore may not need an airspace authorization.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>EXAM TIP  </strong>Do not answer an airspace question based only on what class exists somewhere above the drone. Determine the class at the altitude where the UAS will actually operate.</p>
<h2 id="2-3-class-a-airspace"><strong>2.3 Class A Airspace</strong></h2>
<p>Class A generally begins at 18,000 feet MSL and extends through Flight Level 600. Crewed aircraft in Class A normally operate under Instrument Flight Rules. Because Part 107 operations are ordinarily limited to low altitude, a remote pilot will not normally conduct a small UAS operation in Class A. Nevertheless, the lower boundary—18,000 feet MSL—is a common FAA knowledge-test fact.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>KEY NUMBER  </strong>Class A: 18,000 feet MSL through FL 600.</p>
<h2 id="2-4-class-b-airspace"><strong>2.4 Class B Airspace</strong></h2>
<p>Class B surrounds the nation&#x27;s busiest airports. Its dimensions are individually tailored and often resemble an upside-down wedding cake, with a surface area around the primary airport and shelves extending outward at higher altitudes. Class B generally extends from the surface to about 10,000 feet MSL, although the exact configuration must be read from the chart.</p>
<p>A Part 107 remote pilot must obtain FAA authorization before operating within Class B airspace. On sectional charts, Class B boundaries are shown with solid blue lines. Altitude numbers printed near the boundary identify the floor and ceiling of individual shelves.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>EXAM TIP  </strong>Solid blue = Class B. Do not assume the surface is Class B everywhere under a shelf; read the floor/ceiling figures.</p>
<h2 id="2-5-class-c-airspace"><strong>2.5 Class C Airspace</strong></h2>
<p>Class C normally surrounds airports with an operating control tower and radar approach control. A typical Class C has a 5 NM surface core and a 10 NM shelf, with the upper limit about 4,000 feet above airport elevation. Actual dimensions vary and must be read from the chart.</p>
<p>Class C boundaries are shown by solid magenta lines. Part 107 operations within Class C require FAA airspace authorization.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>KEY NUMBERS  </strong>Typical Class C: 5 NM surface core, 10 NM outer shelf, top about 4,000 feet above airport elevation.</p>
<h2 id="2-6-class-d-airspace"><strong>2.6 Class D Airspace</strong></h2>
<p>Class D generally surrounds airports with an operating control tower. It commonly extends from the surface to approximately 2,500 feet above airport elevation, but its lateral and vertical dimensions are tailored to the airport. The course presentation correctly cautions against assuming every Class D is exactly four nautical miles in radius.</p>
<p>Class D is depicted with a dashed blue boundary. Part 107 operations within Class D require FAA authorization.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>EXAM TIP  </strong>Dashed blue = Class D surface area. Look for the boxed ceiling number on the sectional rather than assuming a standard top.</p>
<h2 id="2-7-class-e-airspace"><strong>2.7 Class E Airspace</strong></h2>
<p>Class E is controlled airspace that is not Class A, B, C, or D. It can begin at the surface, at 700 feet AGL, at 1,200 feet AGL, or at another designated altitude. This makes Class E one of the most frequently misunderstood classes for new remote pilots.</p>
<p>A faded magenta vignette on a sectional normally identifies an area where Class E begins at 700 feet AGL. In many other areas, Class E begins at 1,200 feet AGL. Dashed magenta identifies Class E airspace that begins at the surface around an airport. That surface Class E is the form most important to Part 107 authorization questions.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>EXAM TIP  </strong>Dashed magenta = Class E to the surface. Faded magenta = Class E begins at 700 feet AGL. For a UAS at 200 feet AGL beneath a 700-foot Class E floor, the aircraft is normally in Class G.</p>
<h2 id="2-8-class-g-airspace"><strong>2.8 Class G Airspace</strong></h2>
<p>Class G is uncontrolled airspace. ATC does not provide the same control services there as in controlled airspace, but FAA regulations still apply. Remote pilots must still comply with Part 107, maintain VLOS, yield right-of-way, remain within operating limitations, and check for TFRs, special-use airspace, and other restrictions.</p>
<p>In many rural locations, Class G extends from the surface to either 700 or 1,200 feet AGL, with Class E above. Since many Part 107 flights occur below 400 feet AGL, Class G is a common operating environment.</p>
<p><strong>MEMORY AID  </strong>Class G = uncontrolled, not unregulated.</p>
