<h2 id="2-10-special-use-and-other-airspace"><strong>2.10 Special-Use and Other Airspace</strong></h2>
<p>A sectional chart may show areas created for security, military training, hazardous activity, or other special purposes. These areas require the remote pilot to look beyond the basic B-C-D-E-G classification.</p>
<h3 id="prohibited-areas"><strong>Prohibited Areas</strong></h3>
<p>Aircraft operations are prohibited unless specifically authorized. These areas protect national security or other highly sensitive locations.</p>
<h3 id="restricted-areas"><strong>Restricted Areas</strong></h3>
<p>Contain unusual, often invisible hazards such as artillery firing or missile activity. Operations may be restricted when the area is active; pilots must determine status and controlling/using agency requirements.</p>
<h3 id="warning-areas"><strong>Warning Areas</strong></h3>
<p>Similar in purpose to restricted areas but located over domestic or international waters where the United States does not have sole jurisdiction.</p>
<h3 id="military-operations-areas-moas"><strong>Military Operations Areas (MOAs)</strong></h3>
<p>Separate certain military training activities from IFR traffic. Military aircraft may maneuver rapidly and at high speeds. UAS pilots should exercise extreme caution and determine whether the MOA is active.</p>
<h3 id="alert-areas"><strong>Alert Areas</strong></h3>
<p>Identify locations with a high volume of pilot training or unusual aeronautical activity. All pilots share responsibility for collision avoidance.</p>
<h3 id="controlled-firing-areas"><strong>Controlled Firing Areas</strong></h3>
<p>Activities are suspended when a spotter or other means detects an aircraft approaching the area; CFAs are generally not charted.</p>
<h3 id="military-training-routes"><strong>Military Training Routes</strong></h3>
<p>Routes used by military aircraft for low-level, often high-speed training. A UAS operator near an MTR should expect the possibility of fast aircraft at relatively low altitude.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>REAL-WORLD POINT  </strong>A legal Class G operating site can still be a poor place to fly if it lies under an active military route, near a wildfire TFR, or in an area with frequent low-level aircraft.</p>
<h2 id="2-11-notams-time-critical-information"><strong>2.11 NOTAMs: Time-Critical Information</strong></h2>
<p>NOTAMs—Notices to Air Missions—provide time-critical information that may not yet appear on normal charts or publications. The course material emphasizes checking NOTAMs before every flight because the operating environment can change after a sectional chart was published.</p>
<p>NOTAM information can include runway or taxiway closures, lighting or navigation-aid outages, construction, obstacles, parachute activity, airshows, military activity, and other temporary conditions. Older training materials often emphasize historical NOTAM categories such as NOTAM (D), FDC, Pointer, and Military NOTAMs. Those terms are useful for recognizing older exam-prep material, but the practical skill is finding and interpreting current NOTAM information that affects the planned operation.</p>
<h3 id="fdc-pointer-and-military-information"><strong>FDC, Pointer, and Military Information</strong></h3>
<p>FDC NOTAMs contain regulatory information; TFRs are especially important to UAS pilots. Pointer NOTAMs direct the pilot to another NOTAM containing relevant information. Military NOTAM information can alert pilots to temporary changes or hazards associated with military facilities and operations.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>EXAM TIP  </strong>A sectional chart shows the normal airspace structure. A NOTAM can tell you that today&#x27;s operating environment is different.</p>
<h2 id="2-12-temporary-flight-restrictions-tfrs"><strong>2.12 Temporary Flight Restrictions (TFRs)</strong></h2>
<p>A TFR temporarily restricts aircraft operations in a defined area for a specified purpose and time. TFRs may protect VIP or presidential movements, wildfire and disaster response, space operations, airshows, or qualifying sporting events. A normally legal UAS location can become unavailable when a TFR becomes active.</p>
<p>Wildfire TFRs deserve special attention. Firefighting aircraft may operate low, fast, and repeatedly over the incident. An unauthorized drone can force aerial firefighting operations to stop.</p>
<p><strong>CRITICAL RULE  </strong>An airspace authorization—including LAANC—does not override a TFR. Always check current restrictions before flight.</p>
<h2 id="2-13-faa-airspace-resources"><strong>2.13 FAA Airspace Resources</strong></h2>
<p>No single source gives a remote pilot everything needed for preflight planning. The presentation identifies several complementary FAA resources:</p>
<ul>
<li>Sectional aeronautical charts: normal airspace structure, airports, obstacles, terrain, and charted hazards.</li>
<li>Chart Supplement U.S.: detailed airport and facility information; formerly called the Airport/Facility Directory (A/FD).</li>
<li>NOTAM and Flight Service resources: temporary changes and hazards.</li>
<li>FAA TFR resources: current temporary flight restrictions.</li>
<li>Aeronautical Information Manual (AIM): operational information about the NAS, airport procedures, ATC, communications, and safety.</li>
<li>Advisory Circulars: FAA guidance explaining regulations and recommended procedures.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="2-14-reading-a-vfr-sectional-chart"><strong>2.14 Reading a VFR Sectional Chart</strong></h2>
<p>The FAA knowledge test expects remote pilots to read sectional charts, not merely recognize their appearance. A chart question may ask you to identify airspace at a point, determine the floor or ceiling of controlled airspace, locate an airport from latitude and longitude, identify an obstruction, or recognize a special-use area.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>CHART WORKFLOW  </strong>Locate the point → determine the planned altitude → identify boundary colors/line styles → read nearby altitude labels → check special-use airspace and notes → determine whether authorization or another restriction applies.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/airspace-classification.jpg"><img src="/assets/part107/textbook/airspace-classification.jpg" alt="Cross-section of airspace classes A, B, C, D, E, and G." loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Course presentation figure: airspace classification example (source slide 56).</p><p><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/airspace-classification.jpg">Open original figure at full size</a></p>
<p><strong>Figure description:</strong> The diagram places Class A above 18,000 feet MSL, with its upper boundary labeled FL600. Class B is drawn as stacked shelves around a large airport, Class C as two shelves, and Class D as a smaller surface column. Class E fills much of the controlled airspace between these areas. Class G is shown near the ground where controlled airspace does not extend to the surface. This is a schematic teaching diagram, not a chart for determining airspace at a real location.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="2-15-latitude-longitude-and-topography"><strong>2.15 Latitude, Longitude, and Topography</strong></h2>
<p>A geographic grid of latitude and longitude allows any point on Earth to be described. Latitude lines run east-west and measure distance north or south of the Equator. Longitude lines run north-south and measure distance east or west of the Prime Meridian at Greenwich.</p>
<p>Coordinates may be expressed in degrees, minutes, and seconds. One degree contains 60 minutes and one minute contains 60 seconds. Sectional-chart questions may require you to estimate a location by reading this grid.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>KEY NUMBERS  </strong>1 degree = 60 minutes. 1 minute = 60 seconds.</p>
<p>Topography matters operationally as well. Terrain can block line of sight and radio links, create local wind effects, and complicate the requirement to remain within the allowable height above the ground. Towers and other obstructions can also be difficult to see from a distance.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/sectional-chart.png"><img src="/assets/part107/textbook/sectional-chart.png" alt="Sectional-chart training example showing terrain, airports, and overlapping airspace boundaries." loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Course presentation topography/sectional-chart example (source slide 54).</p><p><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/sectional-chart.png">Open original figure at full size</a></p>
<p><strong>Figure description:</strong> This dense chart excerpt illustrates why a pilot must examine boundary colors and line styles, airport symbols, altitude labels, and terrain together. Use the chart workflow in the preceding text and the airspace comparison table in section 2.9. Open the original figure to magnify individual labels; this historical illustration is not a current navigation chart.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="2-16-airport-operations"><strong>2.16 Airport Operations</strong></h2>
<p>Remote pilots operating near airports must understand how crewed aircraft use the airport environment. Part 107 aircraft may not interfere with airport operations or traffic patterns, and small UAS must always yield right-of-way to other aircraft.</p>
<p>A standard traffic pattern normally uses left turns and consists of departure/upwind, crosswind, downwind, base, and final legs. Some airports use right traffic. Pattern altitude varies, but the course material notes that many traffic patterns are approximately 800 or 1,000 feet AGL.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>REAL-WORLD POINT  </strong>A crewed aircraft can be below normal pattern altitude while taking off, landing, practicing an approach, flying a helicopter route, conducting agricultural work, or responding to an emergency. Never treat pattern altitude as a protective floor for UAS operations.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/airport-traffic-pattern.jpg"><img src="/assets/part107/textbook/airport-traffic-pattern.jpg" alt="Airport diagram comparing left-hand and right-hand traffic patterns." loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Course presentation airport traffic-pattern figure (source slide 57).</p><p><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/airport-traffic-pattern.jpg">Open original figure at full size</a></p>
<p><strong>Figure description:</strong> The pattern legs are departure, crosswind, downwind, base, and final. Two rectangular circuits illustrate turns on opposite sides of a runway. A segmented circle, traffic-pattern indicators, landing-direction indicator, wind cone, and landing runway indicators are also shown. The marked no-transgression zones are beside the runway ends. Read the surrounding airport-operations text for the role of these indicators.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="2-17-runway-numbers-markings-and-signs"><strong>2.17 Runway Numbers, Markings, and Signs</strong></h2>
<p>Runway numbers are based on the runway&#x27;s magnetic direction, rounded to the nearest ten degrees and with the final zero omitted. A runway aligned approximately 180 degrees magnetic is Runway 18; the opposite direction is approximately 360 degrees and is Runway 36. Parallel runways use L, C, or R for left, center, or right.</p>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>EXAM TIP  </strong>Runway 18 points approximately south; Runway 36 points approximately north. Reciprocal runway numbers normally differ by 18.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/runway-markings.jpg"><img src="/assets/part107/textbook/runway-markings.jpg" alt="Illustrated Runway 23 threshold, centerline, edge markings, and adjacent taxiway." loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Course presentation runway-marking example (source slide 59).</p><p><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/runway-markings.jpg">Open original figure at full size</a></p>
<p><strong>Figure description:</strong> The runway illustration labels the threshold with the number 23. White threshold stripes, a dashed white centerline, and white runway edge lines distinguish the runway from the adjacent taxiway, which uses yellow markings. The preceding text explains runway numbering and reciprocal directions.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="2-18-security-identification-display-areas-sida"><strong>2.18 Security Identification Display Areas (SIDA)</strong></h2>
<p>Some airport areas used for passenger or cargo functions are designated Security Identification Display Areas. Individuals in these areas must display airport-issued or other approved identification and comply with airport security requirements. For a UAS operator working on an airport, airspace authorization is only one part of the problem; physical access and airport security procedures may also apply.</p>
<h2 id="2-19-collision-avoidance"><strong>2.19 Collision Avoidance</strong></h2>
<p>The course presentation emphasizes that many midair collisions occur in good visibility. Clear weather can increase traffic and can create a false sense of security. Effective scanning uses a series of short, regularly spaced eye movements rather than a continuous sweep.</p>
<p>A particularly important collision cue is relative motion. An aircraft on a collision course may appear nearly stationary in the field of view while growing larger. If another aircraft remains at the same relative bearing and its apparent size increases, treat the situation as a serious conflict.</p>
<p>When two crewed aircraft approach head-on, each normally alters course to the right. For UAS operations, however, the more fundamental Part 107 rule is simpler: the small UAS must yield to other aircraft and remain well clear.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/approaching-aircraft.jpg"><img src="/assets/part107/textbook/approaching-aircraft.jpg" alt="A crewed aircraft approaching nearly head-on over mountainous terrain." loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Course presentation collision-avoidance example (source slide 64).</p><p><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/approaching-aircraft.jpg">Open original figure at full size</a></p>
<p><strong>Figure description:</strong> The view illustrates the need to detect an approaching aircraft and yield. The accompanying text explains that an aircraft with little apparent relative movement that grows larger can be on a collision course.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>MEMORY AID  </strong>Little or no relative movement + increasing apparent size = possible collision course.</p>
<h2 id="2-20-inflight-hazards"><strong>2.20 Inflight Hazards</strong></h2>
<p>Hazards around a UAS operating area include wildlife, towers, guy wires, terrain, low-flying aircraft, and temporary aviation activity. The course presentation notes that guy wires can extend a considerable horizontal distance from a tower and may be extremely difficult to see.</p>
<p>Wildlife strikes are not only a crewed-aircraft concern. Birds may attack or investigate UAS, and the remote PIC should avoid operating in a manner that creates a collision hazard or disturbs protected wildlife. The operating area should be continually reassessed rather than treated as safe simply because the preflight check was completed.</p>
<h2 id="2-21-chapter-summary"><strong>2.21 Chapter Summary</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>The NAS includes the airspace, airports, facilities, procedures, and regulations that support U.S. aviation.</li>
<li>Classes A, B, C, D, and E are controlled; Class G is uncontrolled.</li>
<li>Part 107 requires prior FAA authorization for Class B, C, D, and Class E surface areas designated for an airport.</li>
<li>Determine airspace at the UAS&#x27;s actual altitude; controlled airspace above the aircraft does not automatically mean the aircraft is in it.</li>
<li>Solid blue identifies Class B; solid magenta Class C; dashed blue Class D; dashed magenta Class E surface areas; faded magenta commonly indicates Class E beginning at 700 feet AGL.</li>
<li>Special-use airspace and military activity can create hazards even when the basic airspace class would otherwise allow a flight.</li>
<li>NOTAMs and TFRs can temporarily change the operating environment. LAANC does not override a TFR.</li>
<li>Sectional charts, the Chart Supplement, AIM, Advisory Circulars, and current FAA/Flight Service information work together during preflight planning.</li>
<li>Remote pilots should understand airport traffic patterns, runway numbering, markings, and security restrictions.</li>
<li>UAS must yield to other aircraft. A target with little relative motion that grows larger may be on a collision course.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="2-22-knowledge-check"><strong>2.22 Knowledge Check</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li>Which classes of airspace are considered controlled airspace?</li>
<li>What is the only uncontrolled airspace class used in the United States?</li>
